.TH TUR\-REPL 1 "2026-05-13" "Turmeric 0.x.0" "Turmeric Language Tools"
.SH NAME
tur\-repl \- Turmeric interactive read\-eval\-print loop
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tur repl
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B tur repl
starts an interactive Turmeric session.
Expressions typed at the prompt are parsed, elaborated, and evaluated;
the result is printed on the following line.
Top-level definitions (\fBdefn\fR, \fBdef\fR) persist across expressions
for the lifetime of the session.
.SH META\-COMMANDS
Meta-commands begin with
.B :
and are handled before evaluation.
.TP
.B :help
Print a summary of all meta\-commands.
.TP
.BR :quit ", " :q
Exit the REPL.
Equivalent to
.BR Ctrl\-D .
.TP
.BI :type " expr"
Elaborate
.I expr
and print its inferred type without evaluating.
.TP
.BI :doc " sym"
Print brief documentation for the builtin or user-defined symbol
.IR sym .
.TP
.BI :reload " file"
Read and evaluate the Turmeric source file
.I file
into the current session.
.SH INPUT
.SS Multi-line expressions
If the input contains unbalanced parentheses the REPL reads additional
lines until the expression is complete.
An empty line cancels an incomplete expression.
.SS Editing and history
When built with
.B editline
support (the default on macOS and most Linux distributions) the REPL
offers line editing (arrow keys, \fBCtrl\-A\fR/\fBCtrl\-E\fR/\fBCtrl\-K\fR) and
history (Up/Down arrows).
Without editline, raw
.B fgets
input is used.
.SH OUTPUT
Results are printed using a human-readable representation:
integers as decimal, strings with surrounding double quotes, booleans as
\fBtrue\fR/\fBfalse\fR, nil silently.
Errors are written to
.BR stderr .
ANSI colour is enabled automatically when both
.B stdout
and
.B stderr
are connected to a terminal.
.SH EXAMPLES
Start the REPL and evaluate an expression:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ tur repl
Turmeric v0.x.0  (type :help for help, :quit to exit)
> (+ 1 2)
3
> :quit
.fi
.RE
.PP
Run a script non\-interactively and exit:
.PP
.RS
.nf
printf ':reload myscript.tur\\n:quit\\n' | tur repl 2>/dev/null
.fi
.RE
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B TERM
Used to detect colour-capable terminals.
